<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward II of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1284–1327</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Edward of Caernarfon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from 1307 to 1327, the son of Edward I. His reliance on favourites such as Piers Gaveston and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/despenser-family">Despensers</a> alienated the barons, and his army was routed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a> at Bannockburn in 1314. He was deposed in 1327 by his wife, Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and Roger Mortimer, and was probably murdered at Berkeley Castle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-ii-of-england.jpg" alt="Edward II of England" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tilman2007 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cathedral_Church_of_St_Peter_and_the_Holy_and_Indivisible_Trinity,_Monument_to_Edward_II_of_England_Gloucester_1245952_20230819_0030.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
